    "q-130771": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130771",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-75.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{4}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-75) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
